* - This is called wildcard, basically it is just select all same type of file / match multiple files, e.g. rm *.txt
directory means folder
Absolute path: Starts from the root / and spells out the entire route to a file, no matter where you currently are. Example- /home/kali/Documents/testingnow.txt
Relative path: Starts from wherever you currently are. Example- Documents/testingnow.txt
| (pipe) → takes the output of the left command and feeds it as input to the command on the right
Terminal is just the window where we type commands. Shell is the program that reads those commands and understands them (bridge between human & kernel). Bash is one type of shell or program. Other shells are: sh, bash, zsh, ksh etc  
Binaries are files that are executable. Their path usually: /usr/bin or usr/sbin. Some example: ls, cat, ifconfig, aircrack-ng etc
using sudo before a command means giving root privilege to that command, need password for it
